        "content": "# Environment Production Workflow\n\n## 1. Establish the game\n\nInfer the player fantasy, camera, traversal, world size, critical route,\ninteraction or combat needs, landmark, hardware target, and quality preset.\nChoose strong defaults for an underspecified ambitious request.\n\n## 2. Compose before detail\n\nSet a player route, foreground frame, midground subject, background landmark,\nheight hierarchy, biome regions, water flow, spawn, and showcase view. A world\nis designed around decisions, not merely scattered.\n\n## 3. Natural foundation\n\nUse procedural systems for geological massing, erosion, rivers, lakes,\nmoisture, snow, biomes, vegetation, water, atmosphere, weather, lighting, and\npost-processing. Use authored tools for villages, roads, bridges, ruins,\ndungeons, props, gameplay objects, and hand-made landmarks.\n\n## 4. Gameplay spaces\n\nCreate a valid player spawn, continuous walkable ground, readable entrances,\nhuman scale, deliberate elevation change, orientation landmarks, points of\ninterest, and routes through or around cliffs and water. Do not let procedural\nterrain create inaccessible objectives.\n\n## 5. Dress and grade\n\nUse ecological distribution and surface-specific dressing. Vary age, scale,\ncondition, and orientation. Add ground detail nearby and structure at distance.\nThen set time, subject light, exposure, cloud placement, fog, and color so the\nroute reads without hiding work.\n\n## 6. Iterate\n\nCapture, compare, identify the top three visual deltas, fix them, and capture\nagain. Continue through refinement rather than stopping after first generation.\n",
