        "id": "quality-pillars",
        "title": "AAA Environment Quality Pillars",
        "path": "aaa-game-worldbuilding/references/quality-pillars.md",
        "content": "# AAA Environment Quality Pillars\n\n## Geometry, not flat illusion\n\nNear-camera terrain needs geological breaks, streambeds, root flare, rocks,\ndebris, and surface transitions that read in silhouette. Do not treat a color\nmap, smooth hero cliff, flat water plane, or a repeated primitive tree as a\nfinished ecosystem.\n\n## Light transport\n\nShowcase shadows must remain readable. Preserve skylight, bounce color,\nfoliage translucency, environmental tint, local contrast, and subject\nseparation. Never hide unfinished detail in black shadows, crushed exposure,\nor opaque fog.\n\n## Nothing is bare\n\nEvery major surface class needs an intentional occupancy or weathering story:\ncliffs, boulder crevices, meadows, tree bases, water margins, roads, ruins,\nsnow lines, and shorelines. Bare areas are valid only when they support the\ncomposition or gameplay.\n\n## Distance holds\n\nThe horizon needs layered terrain, forest structure, atmospheric depth, and\nlandmarks. Do not use fog as a blanket to conceal absent distant content.\n\n## Art direction\n\nGeneration serves composition, color script, visual hierarchy, mood,\nlandmarks, and traversal. A technically complex but random-looking scene is\nnot finished.\n\n## The world moves\n\nUse wind, water, clouds, snow, pollen, leaves, fog, and ambient movers where\nthe active tools support them. Motion should reinforce scale and mood without\nturning the frame into noise.\n",
